<article class="enc-article">
<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jacob Kettler</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1610">1610</a>–<a href="/years/1682">1682</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Duke Jacob of Courland, Jacob, Duke of Courland</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jacob Kettler was Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a> and Semigallia from 1642 to 1682, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of Poland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. Influenced by mercantilist ideas, he built ironworks, shipyards and a large merchant fleet, and founded colonies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">Gambia</a> River and on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tobago">Tobago</a>, making Courland one of the smallest states with overseas possessions. The Swedish invasion of 1658 ruined much of his work.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jacob-kettler.jpg" alt="Jacob Kettler" width="320" height="388" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jacob_Kettler.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jacob Kettler was born on 28 October 1610 in Kuldīga (Goldingen), the son of Wilhelm Kettler and Duchess Sophie of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-frederick-duke-of-prussia">Albert Frederick, Duke of Prussia</a>. He was a godson of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James I of England</a>. While his father was exiled from the duchy, Jacob lived at the courts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Stettin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, studied at Rostock and at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>, and sympathised with the ideas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercantilism">mercantilism</a>. In 1634 he made a grand tour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, visiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>, where he studied shipbuilding. He became co-ruler of the duchy in 1638 and sole duke in 1642.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Under Kettler the duchy traded with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, Portugal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. He began large-scale reforms of the economy, improved agriculture, opened many manufactures and started a shipbuilding industry, and he founded the Fleet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a> and Semigallia, a navy and merchant fleet. The duchy became de facto independent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Polish crown</a>, since all contracts with foreign powers were signed as between independent states, and after 1646 the duke controlled its customs administration.</p><p>In 1651 he sent a fleet to build Fort Jacob on the Gambia River, on an island later known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kunta-kinteh-island">Kunta Kinteh Island</a>. In 1654 he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tobago">Tobago</a> with forces carried on the ship Das Wappen der Herzogin von Kurland, which had 45 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a> and carried 25 officers, 124 Courlander soldiers and eighty families of Latvian colonists, and the colony was named Neu Kurland. In 1651 he also offered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-x">Pope Innocent X</a> 40 warships and 24,000 soldiers and sailors to conquer land in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, in return for the commercial profits. The plan was dropped in 1655 with the pope's death.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-northern-war">Second Northern War</a> the Swedes took the duke prisoner and held him and his family in Riga and later at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivangorod">Ivangorod</a> from 1658 to 1660. His colonies were attacked and lost and his fleet was destroyed. After the war he rebuilt the fleet and retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tobago">Tobago</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, and for the rest of his reign he tried, only partly successfully, to regain the earlier level of wealth. He died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jelgava">Mitau</a> (Jelgava) on 1 January 1682. He is remembered as a fair ruler who gave opportunities to Latvian peasants and knew the Latvian language.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jacob Kettler” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Duke Jacob Kettler</b> of the Duchy of Courland and Semigallia, who built a merchant fleet, acquired St Andrew's Island in the Gambia River from local rulers in 1651.</blockquote><a href="/state/courland-gambia">Courland Gambia</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Duke Jacob Kettler</b> of the Duchy of Courland and Semigallia sent settlers to Tobago in 1654 aboard the ship Das Wappen der Herzogin von Kurland.</blockquote><a href="/state/courland-tobago">Courland Tobago</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Duke Jacob Kettler</b> (1642-1682) built ironworks, shipyards and a merchant fleet, and founded colonies at Courland Gambia and Courland Tobago.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-courland-and-semigallia">Duchy of Courland and Semigallia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kuldīga, in what is now <a href="/countries/latvia">Latvia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jelgava">Jelgava</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/latvia">Latvia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=56.97,21.97&amp;year=1656" aria-label="Kuldīga on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Jacob Kettler"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4627 5237h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4627 5237h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=56.97,21.97&amp;year=1656">What was here in 1656? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
